WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.500
My name is Alessio Figalli, and I'm professor of Mathematics at Zurich.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:15.060
The optimal transport problem consists of finding the cheapest possible way

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.800
Of transporting resources from one place to another.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:23.380
This problem comes form economics, but it turns out to have many impications in mathematics.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:28.060
In particular, one possible application is to study how the shape of crystals

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:31.180
change under the action of exterior forces.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.960
The basic idea is that you can take

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:41.460
The ideal shape of a crystal, the one let's say equations predict

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:46.800
But when you don't take into account all the exterior forces acting on it

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.800
then you can take the real shape of the crystal where forces are acting

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:58.940
and you can try to transport the invisible particles that are composing these crystals

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:02.300
from the ideal configuration to the real configuraiton

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:09.600
and by understanding how these particles move you can understand a lot about the shape.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.760
In the same way you can use the principle to understand the evolution of clouds.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.720
The basic principle in this case is that

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.100
If you look at the cloud in a set instance of time and then at the next instance of time

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:29.380
particles have moved. And if you want to understand how the particles have moved

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:34.460
again you can think there is an optimal transport behind it

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:43.040
and by using this intuition you can actually get very good models.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.460
There is no real universal way to how you approach a problem

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:50.400
Every problem has it's own specifics.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.100
Now if the problem come's from geometry, for instance

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:59.320
when you study the shape of soap bubbles you have a lot of geometric intuition because you can see

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:04.860
the geometric object, you can draw pictures, and let's say by just

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:09.860
looking around you and doing experiments and looking around you you can get a lot of ideas

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.860
of what theorem could be true

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:15.280
and so you have a clear path to follow

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:18.000
The same thing is with problems that come from physics

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.200
you will have physics intuition about what you want to do

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:26.740
But, this being said, at some point you have to start using mathematics

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:30.560
and using analysis which means you really have to work with formulas.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:34.900
And at that moment, it's a completely different world

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:39.220
You just hvae your pen, your piece of paper, and your imagination

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:46.940
to try to make a geometric intuition a physic intuition appear in the formulas

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:51.340
and so being able to prove to formulas

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:56.160
that the equation your studying really model the phenomena's you want to describe

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:01.760
and that is really something which is difficult to explain

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.860
but it's beautiful it's like art, very creative.

